1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,750
Jump Game II — LeetCode 45

2
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:04,600
Problem: nums[i] is the max jump from i, and the end is always reachable — return the fewest jumps to get there.

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,400
Example: nums = [2, 3, 1, 1, 4]

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:10,100
Jump 0 → 1 → 4 — that's 2 jumps

5
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:14,650
Hint: think in levels, like ripples — one jump reaches a range of indices, two jumps reach everything that range can

6
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:19,550
Approach: greedy — farthest is how far the next jump can go, curEnd is the boundary of the current jump; reaching the boundary forces a jump

7
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:24,350
Start: farthest and curEnd both at 0

8
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:26,680
i = 0: farthest = 0 + 2 = 2

9
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:29,270
i equals curEnd → take a jump; curEnd advances to 2

10
00:00:29,270 --> 00:00:31,060
i = 1: farthest = max(2, 1 + 3) = 4

11
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:32,750
i is not at the boundary yet, keep going

12
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:33,990
i = 2: farthest stays 4

13
00:00:33,990 --> 00:00:36,240
i equals curEnd again → second jump; curEnd advances to 4, the end

14
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,320
i = 3: not at the boundary, no jump

15
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:40,670
curEnd reached the end → 2 jumps

16
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:44,760
Each level is one jump, so the fewest is 2. Time O(n), space O(1)

17
